{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EC5Yp8iQK9Nr6MNAjLr7cg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — ec_hte_llm_annotation\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n5\t- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026; commitment deadline August 2, 2026\n6\t- Format: Long paper, 8 pages + unlimited references\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n9\t\n10\t1. **Bias Mechanism Discovery**: When LLM misclassification rates for an effect modifier vary across subgroups, applying a global (pooled) correction to subgroup-level CATE estimates introduces systematic bias — over-correcting low-error subgroups and under-correcting high-error ones. In realistic settings, this \"debiasing\" can worsen CATE bias by 2-3x (median across configurations), with amplification up to 5.9x for K=4 subgroups; when naive bias is near zero, global correction creates bias where none existed. For the difference-in-means estimator, the amplification magnitude does NOT monotonically increase with K (K=2 extreme: up to 2.86× vs K=4 extreme: up to 2.41×); however, with nonlinear estimators such as CausalForest, more subgroups amplify the effect substantially (K=4/extreme: up to 5.9×) due to compounding estimation noise in tree-based CATE. Global correction increases subgroup-level bias in ~50% of the parameter space.\n11\t\n12\t2. **EC-HTE Solution**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction (EC-HTE) substantially reduces subgroup-level bias when misclassification heterogeneity is sufficiently high, while controlling estimation variance through partial pooling. HB provides meaningful RMSE reduction by borrowing strength across groups (classic James-Stein shrinkage). We provide precise crossover thresholds: EC-HTE outperforms global correction in RMSE with as few as 250 expert labels (K=2, high heterogeneity), scaling to ~1000 for K=4. **Boundary condition**: when expert labels per subgroup < ~25, shrinkage can be harmful — stratified MLE is preferred. **EC-HTE is most valuable precisely when it is most needed — the diagnostic criterion (Claim 3) identifies these scenarios.** **CONFIRMED** by exp-003 full 100 MC (1200 runs, 0 errors). HB crossover at ~11 experts/subgroup; ≥25 recommended for robust performance. Demonstrated with both difference-in-means and CausalForestDML (exp-011: consistent bias reduction pattern, ec_hte_forest |bias|=0.06 vs naive 0.16). CausalForest amplifies the debiasing-hurts effect (up to 5.9x in K=4/extreme) due to nonlinear CATE estimation.\n13\t\n14\t3. **Diagnostic Criterion**: The product of misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity across subgroups provides a computable diagnostic that simultaneously predicts when global correction is harmful (Claim 1) and when the expert label investment for stratified correction is worthwhile (connecting to the budget guidelines from Claim 2). This criterion has standalone value independent of EC-HTE — even without adopting EC-HTE, knowing when global correction backfires is practically actionable.\n15\t\n16\t## Story Spine\n17\t\n18\t- **Problem**: NLP researchers increasingly use LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis across computational social science (CSS) — e.g., sentiment framing moderates message effectiveness, hate speech type moderates intervention impact. LLM misclassification is non-random — error rates vary by subgroup (Ashwin et al., 2025: error varies by refugee status, gender, education; documented across CSS tasks including sentiment, stance, toxicity). Current PPI/DSL corrections apply global rectifiers assuming homogeneous error.\n19\t\n20\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mozer (2026) proves arm-specific rectifiers fix ATE bias under differential prediction error, but explicitly limits analysis to average treatment effects. PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) and DSL (Egami et al., 2024) correct population-level estimates. PPCI (Sui et al., 2026) extends PPI to conditional functionals but corrects outcome-side measurement, not covariate-side. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression, not CATE. **No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure.**\n21\t\n22\t- **Our framing**: We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism — measurement-error × causal-heterogeneity confounding. When a pooled confusion matrix is applied to correct subgroup-level CATEs, it misattributes prediction noise across subgroups with different error rates, producing bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. This is a problem-level discovery, not a technique-level extension. EC-HTE solves it via hierarchical Bayesian stratified correction with honest causal forest integration. **Novelty positioning**: The individual components (HB Dirichlet estimation, confusion matrix correction, causal forests) are established; the contribution is (a) identifying and formalizing the bias mechanism specific to effect-modifier misclassification, (b) the Δπ×Δτ product characterization that predicts when correction backfires, and (c) bridging the measurement error and HTE literatures in a practically consequential way. Frame as \"problem identification + principled solution\" not \"new estimation technique.\"\n23\t\n24\t- **Headline result template**: \"On semi-synthetic CSS benchmarks, global correction worsens subgroup CATE bias by 2-3x (median), with amplification up to 5.9x for K=4 subgroups; EC-HTE reduces bias to near-zero while maintaining 92-97% coverage, with as few as 250 expert labels. Combined with PPCI, the composition achieves near-zero bias (0.005) even when both covariate and outcome errors are present.\"\n25\t\n26\t- **Oracle framing**: When reporting DGP C results where ec_hte_ppci outperforms oracle, always use \"covariate-oracle\" (true Z + observed Y), never unqualified \"oracle\". Frame as \"composite correction is necessary and sufficient when both error types coexist\" — NOT \"beats oracle\". The result is by construction (ec_hte_ppci corrects both error types; covariate-oracle only corrects one), not surprising.\n27\t\n28\t## Key Contributions (ordered for paper)\n29\t\n30\t1. **Bias decomposition identity**: Exact population-level identity: bias ∝ misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity (Δπ×Δτ product form). NOT a bound — it is an exact identity under A1–A4; finite-sample R² measures MC noise, not bound looseness. Includes \"debiasing can hurt\" proposition with constructive example. Frame as \"bias decomposition\" or \"bias identity\" throughout — never \"bias bound.\" (Theory + Empirical)\n31\t2. **EC-HTE framework**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified misclassification correction + variance-adjusted honest causal forest. (Method)\n32\t3. **Diagnostic criterion**: Computable threshold from pilot data to decide whether stratified correction is needed. (Practical tool)\n33\t4. **EC-HTE + PPCI composition — empirically necessary and sufficient**: EC-HTE and PPCI are empirically necessary and sufficient in composition: neither alone adequately handles dual-error scenarios (DGP C), but their composition reduces bias to near-zero (0.005, a 35× improvement over EC-HTE alone). EC-HTE exploits mixing matrix structure for covariate-side error; PPCI rectifier captures outcome-side error. PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but with higher variance (limited by gold subset size). In Methods, retain full discussion of partial overlap; in Abstract, lead with \"necessary and sufficient in composition.\" (Method + Empirical)\n34\t5. **Comprehensive validation**: Synthetic + semi-synthetic + real-data case study (pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark) with multiple LLMs. (Empirical)\n35\t\n36\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n37\t\n38\t1. **\"Why not just use expert labels directly for CATE?\"** — Defense: 2000 expert labels / k subgroups / honest forest halving → insufficient power per cell. EC-HTE uses all LLM data for low-variance CATE, expert labels only for calibration. Explicitly quantify in experiments.\n39\t\n40\t2. **\"How is this different from PPCI / DSL?\"** — PPCI rectifier is a general-purpose correction that captures any systematic error (covariate + outcome); EC-HTE is a structured correction exploiting mixing matrix structure specifically for covariate-side error. They are complementary: EC-HTE alone is more efficient for covariate error (uses M⁻¹ structure), PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but with higher variance (limited by gold subset). In composition, EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, residual rectifier handles outcome error. DSL applied per-subgroup is a valid baseline but lacks cross-subgroup shrinkage, operates at estimand level rather than measurement model level, and has strictly higher variance under partial pooling. Do NOT say \"DSL cannot do subgroup correction\" — say it lacks the principled shrinkage mechanism.\n41\t\n42\t3. **\"Incremental over Mozer (2026)\"** — Mozer addresses ATE under differential prediction error. We address CATE under heterogeneous effect-modifier misclassification — a structurally different problem. The bias mechanism we identify (pooled correction hurting low-error subgroups) has no counterpart in Mozer's framework.\n43\t\n44\t4. **\"How sensitive to subgroup definition?\"** — Hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling makes the method robust: with well-defined subgroups, full stratification; with noisy subgroups, automatic shrinkage toward global. Include robustness analysis.\n45\t\n46\t5. **\"Real-world expert label cost\"** — exp-006 provides precise RMSE crossover points: K=2/extreme n_expert=250, K=4/extreme n=1000, K=4/moderate n=2000, K=2/moderate never crosses (global always better on RMSE). Paper needs \"Expert Budget Recommendation\" subsection with per-K guidelines. Key message: when diagnostic criterion flags high heterogeneity (extreme regime), EC-HTE is worth the investment at moderate budgets; when heterogeneity is low, global correction is the safer choice. This honest framing connects Claim 2 (EC-HTE method) to Claim 3 (diagnostic criterion) — the diagnostic decides the correction strategy.\n47\t\n48\t6. **\"How does this differ from Carroll et al. (2006) / classical measurement error?\"** — The qualitative insight that misspecified corrections can amplify MSE is classical (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3). Our specific contributions beyond this are: (a) the quantitative Δπ×Δτ product form for CATE estimation with effect-modifier misclassification, (b) demonstrating this failure mode occurs *by default* in standard LLM annotation workflows, and (c) providing a computable diagnostic and correction framework. Must explicitly cite and contextualize: Fuller (1987), Carroll et al. (2006 Ch. 4), Gustafson (2003), Hu & Schennach (2008), Imai & Yamamoto (2010). Also cite Bayesian annotation models: Dawid & Skene, Passonneau & Carpenter (2014), Hovy et al. (2013). **Bibliography target: 30-35 references** spanning measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator (Tornberg 2024, Gilardi et al. 2023, Ziems et al. 2024), HTE methods (Wager & Athey 2018, Kennedy 2023, Chernozhukov et al. 2018).\n49\t\n50\t7. **\"Methodological novelty is modest\"** — Acknowledge explicitly. Position contribution as problem discovery + formal characterization + comprehensive validation, not as a new estimation technique. HB partial pooling provides meaningful RMSE reduction for moderate subgroups but is NOT \"never worse than global\" — it is a James-Stein shrinkage estimator with known boundary conditions. **Recommended condition**: n_expert per subgroup ≥ ~25 (e.g., n_expert ≥ 100 for K=4 with 12% minority). Below this, stratified MLE is preferred. This honest limitation strengthens credibility. State conditions explicitly using per-subgroup formulation, not just total n_expert.\n51\t\n52\t8. **\"Why no figures?\"** — Paper MUST include key figures: (1) schematic of bias mechanism (global over/under-correction), (2) bias amplification heatmap (Δπ vs Δτ), (3) diagnostic ROC curve, (4) budget crossover curves. Move P2 experiments (exp-008, exp-009) to appendix to free space.\n53\t\n54\t## Venue Fit\n55\t\n56\tEMNLP fit is strong: core NLP application (LLM annotation quality), computational social science use cases (sentiment analysis, hate speech detection, refugee interview coding), direct comparison with NeurIPS/EMNLP published methods (DSL, PPI, benchmarking). Methodology is causal inference but framed through NLP lens. Potential reviewer pool: CL+CSS track. **NOTE**: Framing broadened from \"political-science\" to \"CSS\" (D004b) — TweetEval stance tasks too small (N<1000), sentiment (N=45K) usable but not political science. CSS is more accurate and equally strong for EMNLP.\n57\t\n58\tRisk: pure statistics reviewers may want stronger theoretical contribution (convergence rates, minimax bounds). Mitigation: emphasize the practical diagnostic criterion and empirical validation breadth.\n59\t\n60\t## Writing Requirements (Dream Round 2 consensus)\n61\t\n62\t1. **\"Bias decomposition\" NOT \"bias bound\"** — Theorem 1 is an exact identity at population level. R² < 1.0 in verification reflects finite-sample MC noise, not bound looseness.\n63\t2. **Bibliography: 30-35 references minimum** — Must span 5 literatures: classical measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator, CSS. Critical citations: Fuller 1987, Wager & Athey 2018, Imai & Yamamoto 2010.\n64\t3. **3+ figures in main text** — (a) bias mechanism schematic, (b) Δπ×Δτ amplification heatmap, (c) diagnostic ROC curve, (d) budget crossover curves. Move exp-008/exp-009 tables to appendix.\n65\t4. **Semi-synthetic DGP fully specified** — outcome generation, treatment assignment, CATE injection mechanism.\n66\t5. **Real-data framed as pattern consistency** — NOT ground-truth benchmark. No bias/RMSE against unknown true CATEs.\n67\t6. **EC-HTE advantage is conditional, not universal** — ec_hte dominates when misclassification heterogeneity is high (extreme regime) OR n_expert is large (≥500). In moderate regime with small n_expert (~100), global correction can outperform ec_hte because per-subgroup CM estimation noise exceeds the benefit of stratification. This connects to Claim 3: the diagnostic criterion (Δπ×Δτ) predicts when EC-HTE is needed. Unified condition: n_expert/K ≥ ~25-50 experts per subgroup.\n68\t11. **RMSE tradeoff must be disclosed** — ec_hte has higher RMSE than global/naive in K=4/n≤100 configs (up to 3.5x in extreme cases) due to noisy per-subgroup CM estimation. Bias is lower but variance is higher. Must report both bias AND RMSE; include bias-RMSE tradeoff analysis. At n_expert≥500, ec_hte RMSE becomes competitive.\n69\t12. **K-dependence of debiasing-hurts** — DIM estimator: K=2 extreme ~2.86×, K=4 extreme ~2.41× (does NOT monotonically increase with K). CausalForest: K=4 extreme up to 5.9× (nonlinear estimator amplifies with more subgroups). Report both patterns; do NOT claim \"more subgroups → always worse\" without qualifying the estimator.\n70\t7. **Confusion matrix invertibility** — State det(Ĉ_k) ≠ 0 assumption explicitly.\n71\t8. **Diagnostic AUC cross-validated** — Leave-one-setting-out or held-out evaluation, not in-sample.\n72\t9. **Hyperprior values stated + sensitivity analysis** — Gamma(α, β) for Dirichlet concentration; report sensitivity to 2× changes.\n73\t10. **PPCI complementarity elevated** — Highlight in abstract and contributions. Frame as \"complementary\" not \"orthogonal\": EC-HTE is structured covariate-error correction; PPCI rectifier is general residual correction. Composition = EC-HTE handles structured part + rectifier handles remainder (primarily outcome error).\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86283adc-5998-4c8b-8d0b-bff1cafdd1f3","uuid":"e68a8e1a-87b3-44db-aff4-03d4d2078be2","timestamp":"2026-05-23T20:04:51.785Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T193110/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — ec_hte_llm_annotation\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026; commitment deadline August 2, 2026\n- Format: Long paper, 8 pages + unlimited references\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n1. **Bias Mechanism Discovery**: When LLM misclassification rates for an effect modifier vary across subgroups, applying a global (pooled) correction to subgroup-level CATE estimates introduces systematic bias — over-correcting low-error subgroups and under-correcting high-error ones. In realistic settings, this \"debiasing\" can worsen CATE bias by 2-3x (median across configurations), with amplification up to 5.9x for K=4 subgroups; when naive bias is near zero, global correction creates bias where none existed. For the difference-in-means estimator, the amplification magnitude does NOT monotonically increase with K (K=2 extreme: up to 2.86× vs K=4 extreme: up to 2.41×); however, with nonlinear estimators such as CausalForest, more subgroups amplify the effect substantially (K=4/extreme: up to 5.9×) due to compounding estimation noise in tree-based CATE. Global correction increases subgroup-level bias in ~50% of the parameter space.\n\n2. **EC-HTE Solution**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction (EC-HTE) substantially reduces subgroup-level bias when misclassification heterogeneity is sufficiently high, while controlling estimation variance through partial pooling. HB provides meaningful RMSE reduction by borrowing strength across groups (classic James-Stein shrinkage). We provide precise crossover thresholds: EC-HTE outperforms global correction in RMSE with as few as 250 expert labels (K=2, high heterogeneity), scaling to ~1000 for K=4. **Boundary condition**: when expert labels per subgroup < ~25, shrinkage can be harmful — stratified MLE is preferred. **EC-HTE is most valuable precisely when it is most needed — the diagnostic criterion (Claim 3) identifies these scenarios.** **CONFIRMED** by exp-003 full 100 MC (1200 runs, 0 errors). HB crossover at ~11 experts/subgroup; ≥25 recommended for robust performance. Demonstrated with both difference-in-means and CausalForestDML (exp-011: consistent bias reduction pattern, ec_hte_forest |bias|=0.06 vs naive 0.16). CausalForest amplifies the debiasing-hurts effect (up to 5.9x in K=4/extreme) due to nonlinear CATE estimation.\n\n3. **Diagnostic Criterion**: The product of misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity across subgroups provides a computable diagnostic that simultaneously predicts when global correction is harmful (Claim 1) and when the expert label investment for stratified correction is worthwhile (connecting to the budget guidelines from Claim 2). This criterion has standalone value independent of EC-HTE — even without adopting EC-HTE, knowing when global correction backfires is practically actionable.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: NLP researchers increasingly use LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis across computational social science (CSS) — e.g., sentiment framing moderates message effectiveness, hate speech type moderates intervention impact. LLM misclassification is non-random — error rates vary by subgroup (Ashwin et al., 2025: error varies by refugee status, gender, education; documented across CSS tasks including sentiment, stance, toxicity). Current PPI/DSL corrections apply global rectifiers assuming homogeneous error.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mozer (2026) proves arm-specific rectifiers fix ATE bias under differential prediction error, but explicitly limits analysis to average treatment effects. PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) and DSL (Egami et al., 2024) correct population-level estimates. PPCI (Sui et al., 2026) extends PPI to conditional functionals but corrects outcome-side measurement, not covariate-side. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression, not CATE. **No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure.**\n\n- **Our framing**: We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism — measurement-error × causal-heterogeneity confounding. When a pooled confusion matrix is applied to correct subgroup-level CATEs, it misattributes prediction noise across subgroups with different error rates, producing bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. This is a problem-level discovery, not a technique-level extension. EC-HTE solves it via hierarchical Bayesian stratified correction with honest causal forest integration. **Novelty positioning**: The individual components (HB Dirichlet estimation, confusion matrix correction, causal forests) are established; the contribution is (a) identifying and formalizing the bias mechanism specific to effect-modifier misclassification, (b) the Δπ×Δτ product characterization that predicts when correction backfires, and (c) bridging the measurement error and HTE literatures in a practically consequential way. Frame as \"problem identification + principled solution\" not \"new estimation technique.\"\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On semi-synthetic CSS benchmarks, global correction worsens subgroup CATE bias by 2-3x (median), with amplification up to 5.9x for K=4 subgroups; EC-HTE reduces bias to near-zero while maintaining 92-97% coverage, with as few as 250 expert labels. Combined with PPCI, the composition achieves near-zero bias (0.005) even when both covariate and outcome errors are present.\"\n\n- **Oracle framing**: When reporting DGP C results where ec_hte_ppci outperforms oracle, always use \"covariate-oracle\" (true Z + observed Y), never unqualified \"oracle\". Frame as \"composite correction is necessary and sufficient when both error types coexist\" — NOT \"beats oracle\". The result is by construction (ec_hte_ppci corrects both error types; covariate-oracle only corrects one), not surprising.\n\n## Key Contributions (ordered for paper)\n\n1. **Bias decomposition identity**: Exact population-level identity: bias ∝ misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity (Δπ×Δτ product form). NOT a bound — it is an exact identity under A1–A4; finite-sample R² measures MC noise, not bound looseness. Includes \"debiasing can hurt\" proposition with constructive example. Frame as \"bias decomposition\" or \"bias identity\" throughout — never \"bias bound.\" (Theory + Empirical)\n2. **EC-HTE framework**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified misclassification correction + variance-adjusted honest causal forest. (Method)\n3. **Diagnostic criterion**: Computable threshold from pilot data to decide whether stratified correction is needed. (Practical tool)\n4. **EC-HTE + PPCI composition — empirically necessary and sufficient**: EC-HTE and PPCI are empirically necessary and sufficient in composition: neither alone adequately handles dual-error scenarios (DGP C), but their composition reduces bias to near-zero (0.005, a 35× improvement over EC-HTE alone). EC-HTE exploits mixing matrix structure for covariate-side error; PPCI rectifier captures outcome-side error. PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but with higher variance (limited by gold subset size). In Methods, retain full discussion of partial overlap; in Abstract, lead with \"necessary and sufficient in composition.\" (Method + Empirical)\n5. **Comprehensive validation**: Synthetic + semi-synthetic + real-data case study (pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark) with multiple LLMs. (Empirical)\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. **\"Why not just use expert labels directly for CATE?\"** — Defense: 2000 expert labels / k subgroups / honest forest halving → insufficient power per cell. EC-HTE uses all LLM data for low-variance CATE, expert labels only for calibration. Explicitly quantify in experiments.\n\n2. **\"How is this different from PPCI / DSL?\"** — PPCI rectifier is a general-purpose correction that captures any systematic error (covariate + outcome); EC-HTE is a structured correction exploiting mixing matrix structure specifically for covariate-side error. They are complementary: EC-HTE alone is more efficient for covariate error (uses M⁻¹ structure), PPCI alone can partially correct covariate error but with higher variance (limited by gold subset). In composition, EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, residual rectifier handles outcome error. DSL applied per-subgroup is a valid baseline but lacks cross-subgroup shrinkage, operates at estimand level rather than measurement model level, and has strictly higher variance under partial pooling. Do NOT say \"DSL cannot do subgroup correction\" — say it lacks the principled shrinkage mechanism.\n\n3. **\"Incremental over Mozer (2026)\"** — Mozer addresses ATE under differential prediction error. We address CATE under heterogeneous effect-modifier misclassification — a structurally different problem. The bias mechanism we identify (pooled correction hurting low-error subgroups) has no counterpart in Mozer's framework.\n\n4. **\"How sensitive to subgroup definition?\"** — Hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling makes the method robust: with well-defined subgroups, full stratification; with noisy subgroups, automatic shrinkage toward global. Include robustness analysis.\n\n5. **\"Real-world expert label cost\"** — exp-006 provides precise RMSE crossover points: K=2/extreme n_expert=250, K=4/extreme n=1000, K=4/moderate n=2000, K=2/moderate never crosses (global always better on RMSE). Paper needs \"Expert Budget Recommendation\" subsection with per-K guidelines. Key message: when diagnostic criterion flags high heterogeneity (extreme regime), EC-HTE is worth the investment at moderate budgets; when heterogeneity is low, global correction is the safer choice. This honest framing connects Claim 2 (EC-HTE method) to Claim 3 (diagnostic criterion) — the diagnostic decides the correction strategy.\n\n6. **\"How does this differ from Carroll et al. (2006) / classical measurement error?\"** — The qualitative insight that misspecified corrections can amplify MSE is classical (Fuller 1987, Sec. 2.3). Our specific contributions beyond this are: (a) the quantitative Δπ×Δτ product form for CATE estimation with effect-modifier misclassification, (b) demonstrating this failure mode occurs *by default* in standard LLM annotation workflows, and (c) providing a computable diagnostic and correction framework. Must explicitly cite and contextualize: Fuller (1987), Carroll et al. (2006 Ch. 4), Gustafson (2003), Hu & Schennach (2008), Imai & Yamamoto (2010). Also cite Bayesian annotation models: Dawid & Skene, Passonneau & Carpenter (2014), Hovy et al. (2013). **Bibliography target: 30-35 references** spanning measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator (Tornberg 2024, Gilardi et al. 2023, Ziems et al. 2024), HTE methods (Wager & Athey 2018, Kennedy 2023, Chernozhukov et al. 2018).\n\n7. **\"Methodological novelty is modest\"** — Acknowledge explicitly. Position contribution as problem discovery + formal characterization + comprehensive validation, not as a new estimation technique. HB partial pooling provides meaningful RMSE reduction for moderate subgroups but is NOT \"never worse than global\" — it is a James-Stein shrinkage estimator with known boundary conditions. **Recommended condition**: n_expert per subgroup ≥ ~25 (e.g., n_expert ≥ 100 for K=4 with 12% minority). Below this, stratified MLE is preferred. This honest limitation strengthens credibility. State conditions explicitly using per-subgroup formulation, not just total n_expert.\n\n8. **\"Why no figures?\"** — Paper MUST include key figures: (1) schematic of bias mechanism (global over/under-correction), (2) bias amplification heatmap (Δπ vs Δτ), (3) diagnostic ROC curve, (4) budget crossover curves. Move P2 experiments (exp-008, exp-009) to appendix to free space.\n\n## Venue Fit\n\nEMNLP fit is strong: core NLP application (LLM annotation quality), computational social science use cases (sentiment analysis, hate speech detection, refugee interview coding), direct comparison with NeurIPS/EMNLP published methods (DSL, PPI, benchmarking). Methodology is causal inference but framed through NLP lens. Potential reviewer pool: CL+CSS track. **NOTE**: Framing broadened from \"political-science\" to \"CSS\" (D004b) — TweetEval stance tasks too small (N<1000), sentiment (N=45K) usable but not political science. CSS is more accurate and equally strong for EMNLP.\n\nRisk: pure statistics reviewers may want stronger theoretical contribution (convergence rates, minimax bounds). Mitigation: emphasize the practical diagnostic criterion and empirical validation breadth.\n\n## Writing Requirements (Dream Round 2 consensus)\n\n1. **\"Bias decomposition\" NOT \"bias bound\"** — Theorem 1 is an exact identity at population level. R² < 1.0 in verification reflects finite-sample MC noise, not bound looseness.\n2. **Bibliography: 30-35 references minimum** — Must span 5 literatures: classical measurement error, Bayesian estimation, causal inference, LLM-as-annotator, CSS. Critical citations: Fuller 1987, Wager & Athey 2018, Imai & Yamamoto 2010.\n3. **3+ figures in main text** — (a) bias mechanism schematic, (b) Δπ×Δτ amplification heatmap, (c) diagnostic ROC curve, (d) budget crossover curves. Move exp-008/exp-009 tables to appendix.\n4. **Semi-synthetic DGP fully specified** — outcome generation, treatment assignment, CATE injection mechanism.\n5. **Real-data framed as pattern consistency** — NOT ground-truth benchmark. No bias/RMSE against unknown true CATEs.\n6. **EC-HTE advantage is conditional, not universal** — ec_hte dominates when misclassification heterogeneity is high (extreme regime) OR n_expert is large (≥500). In moderate regime with small n_expert (~100), global correction can outperform ec_hte because per-subgroup CM estimation noise exceeds the benefit of stratification. This connects to Claim 3: the diagnostic criterion (Δπ×Δτ) predicts when EC-HTE is needed. Unified condition: n_expert/K ≥ ~25-50 experts per subgroup.\n11. **RMSE tradeoff must be disclosed** — ec_hte has higher RMSE than global/naive in K=4/n≤100 configs (up to 3.5x in extreme cases) due to noisy per-subgroup CM estimation. Bias is lower but variance is higher. Must report both bias AND RMSE; include bias-RMSE tradeoff analysis. At n_expert≥500, ec_hte RMSE becomes competitive.\n12. **K-dependence of debiasing-hurts** — DIM estimator: K=2 extreme ~2.86×, K=4 extreme ~2.41× (does NOT monotonically increase with K). CausalForest: K=4 extreme up to 5.9× (nonlinear estimator amplifies with more subgroups). Report both patterns; do NOT claim \"more subgroups → always worse\" without qualifying the estimator.\n7. **Confusion matrix invertibility** — State det(Ĉ_k) ≠ 0 assumption explicitly.\n8. **Diagnostic AUC cross-validated** — Leave-one-setting-out or held-out evaluation, not in-sample.\n9. **Hyperprior values stated + sensitivity analysis** — Gamma(α, β) for Dirichlet concentration; report sensitivity to 2× changes.\n10. **PPCI complementarity elevated** — Highlight in abstract and contributions. Frame as \"complementary\" not \"orthogonal\": EC-HTE is structured covariate-error correction; PPCI rectifier is general residual correction. Composition = EC-HTE handles structured part + rectifier handles remainder (primarily outcome error).\n","numLines":74,"startLine":1,"totalLines":74}}}
